# 🚀 Complete GCP Testing Guide for PIQC Tool

> **Estimated Cost: $2-5 for complete testing**  
> **Time Required: ~30-45 minutes**

---

## 📖 What You Need to Know First

### What is GCP?
**Google Cloud Platform (GCP)** is like renting computers from Google. Instead of buying expensive hardware, you pay per hour to use Google's computers.

### What is Kubernetes (K8s)?
**Kubernetes** is a system that manages containers (lightweight apps) across multiple computers. Think of it as an orchestra conductor that tells each musician (container) what to do.

### What is GKE?
**Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)** is Google's managed Kubernetes service. Google handles the complex setup; you just use it.

### What is a GPU?
A **GPU** (Graphics Processing Unit) is a special chip that AI models need to run fast. We'll use NVIDIA T4 GPU - the cheapest option.

### What is vLLM?
**vLLM** is software that runs AI language models efficiently. Our tool (PIQC) finds and documents vLLM deployments.

---

## 🛡️ Prerequisites Checklist

Before starting, make sure you have:

- [ ] GCP account with billing enabled (client should have provided access)
- [ ] A modern web browser (Chrome recommended)
- [ ] ~30 minutes of uninterrupted time

---

## 📋 Step-by-Step Guide

### PHASE 1: Access Google Cloud Console

#### Step 1.1: Open Google Cloud Console
1. Go to: **https://console.cloud.google.com**
2. Sign in with the account that has GCP access
3. You should see the Google Cloud Console dashboard

#### Step 1.2: Open Cloud Shell
Cloud Shell is a free terminal (command-line) in your browser - no installation needed!

1. Look at the **top-right corner** of the page
2. Click the icon that looks like `>_` (terminal icon)
3. A black terminal window will open at the bottom
4. Wait for it to say "Welcome to Cloud Shell!"

> 💡 **Tip**: Click the "Open in new window" button to make it full screen

---

### PHASE 2: Set Up Your Project

#### Step 2.1: Check Your Project
Copy and paste this command into Cloud Shell and press Enter:

```bash
gcloud config get-value project
```

This shows your current project ID. If it shows the wrong project, the client needs to tell you the correct project ID.

#### Step 2.2: Set the Correct Project (if needed)
Replace `YOUR_PROJECT_ID` with the actual project ID:

```bash
gcloud config set project YOUR_PROJECT_ID
```

#### Step 2.3: Enable Required Services
These commands turn on the features we need. Run each one:

```bash
# Enable Kubernetes Engine API
gcloud services enable container.googleapis.com

# Enable Compute Engine API  
gcloud services enable compute.googleapis.com
```

> ⏱️ **Wait**: This may take 1-2 minutes

---

### PHASE 3: Create the Kubernetes Cluster (THE EXPENSIVE PART)

> ⚠️ **IMPORTANT**: This step starts billing! We use "Spot" instances which are 60-70% cheaper but can be interrupted.

#### Step 3.1: Create the Cluster
Copy and paste this entire command (it's one command):

```bash
gcloud container clusters create piqc-test \
  --zone=us-central1-a \
  --machine-type=n1-standard-4 \
  --accelerator=type=nvidia-tesla-t4,count=1 \
  --num-nodes=1 \
  --spot \
  --disk-size=50GB \
  --enable-autoscaling \
  --min-nodes=0 \
  --max-nodes=1
```

**What this does:**
- `piqc-test` = name of your cluster
- `us-central1-a` = location (US Central has good GPU availability)
- `n1-standard-4` = computer type (4 CPUs, 15GB RAM)
- `nvidia-tesla-t4` = cheapest GPU (~$0.35/hour with Spot)
- `--spot` = uses cheaper "Spot" pricing

> ⏱️ **Wait**: This takes **5-10 minutes**. You'll see progress messages.

#### Step 3.2: Install GPU Drivers
After the cluster is created, run:

```bash
kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/container-engine-accelerators/master/nvidia-driver-installer/cos/daemonset-preloaded.yaml
```

> ⏱️ **Wait**: Give it 2-3 minutes for drivers to install

#### Step 3.3: Verify GPU is Ready
Check if the GPU is available:

```bash
kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[*].status.allocatable.nvidia\.com/gpu}'
```

✅ **Expected output**: `1` (means 1 GPU is ready)

If you see nothing, wait another minute and try again.

---

### PHASE 4: Deploy a Test AI Model (vLLM)

#### Step 4.1: Create a Namespace
A namespace is like a folder to organize things:

```bash
kubectl create namespace inference
```

#### Step 4.2: Create the vLLM Deployment File
This creates a file with the vLLM configuration:

```bash
cat << 'EOF' > vllm-tinyllama.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: vllm-tinyllama
  namespace: inference
  labels:
    app: vllm-tinyllama
    app.kubernetes.io/name: vllm
    framework: vllm
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: vllm-tinyllama
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: vllm-tinyllama
        app.kubernetes.io/name: vllm
        framework: vllm
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: vllm
        image: vllm/vllm-openai:latest
        args:
          - "--model"
          - "TinyLlama/TinyLlama-1.1B-Chat-v1.0"
          - "--served-model-name"
          - "tinyllama"
          - "--dtype"
          - "float16"
          - "--max-model-len"
          - "2048"
          - "--tensor-parallel-size"
          - "1"
          - "--gpu-memory-utilization"
          - "0.9"
          - "--enable-prefix-caching"
        ports:
          - containerPort: 8000
            name: http
        resources:
          requests:
            nvidia.com/gpu: 1
            cpu: "2"
            memory: "8Gi"
          limits:
            nvidia.com/gpu: 1
            cpu: "4"
            memory: "12Gi"
        readinessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /health
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 120
          periodSeconds: 10
        livenessProbe:
          httpGet:
            path: /health
            port: 8000
          initialDelaySeconds: 180
          periodSeconds: 30
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: vllm-tinyllama
  namespace: inference
spec:
  selector:
    app: vllm-tinyllama
  ports:
    - port: 8000
      targetPort: 8000
      name: http
  type: ClusterIP
EOF
```

#### Step 4.3: Deploy vLLM
```bash
kubectl apply -f vllm-tinyllama.yaml
```

#### Step 4.4: Watch the Deployment
Watch the pod status until it says "Running":

```bash
kubectl get pods -n inference -w
```

You'll see statuses like:
- `Pending` → waiting for resources
- `ContainerCreating` → downloading the AI model
- `Running` → ready! ✅

> ⏱️ **Wait**: This takes **3-5 minutes** (downloading the model)

Press `Ctrl+C` to stop watching once you see "Running"

#### Step 4.5: Verify vLLM is Working
Check the logs to see if the model loaded:

```bash
kubectl logs -n inference -l app=vllm-tinyllama --tail=20
```

Look for a message like: `"INFO: Uvicorn running on http://0.0.0.0:8000"`

---

### PHASE 5: Upload PIQC Tool to Cloud Shell

#### Step 5.1: Create ZIP File (On Your Windows Laptop)

1. Open **File Explorer**
2. Navigate to `C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop`
3. **Right-click** the `ModelSpec` folder
4. Select **"Send to"** → **"Compressed (zipped) folder"**
5. This creates `ModelSpec.zip` on your Desktop

#### Step 5.2: Upload ZIP to Cloud Shell

1. In your Cloud Shell window (bottom of GCP Console)
2. Click the **three dots menu (⋮)** in the top-right corner of Cloud Shell
3. Click **"Upload"**
4. Select `ModelSpec.zip` from your Desktop
5. Wait for upload to complete (you'll see a progress bar)

#### Step 5.3: Extract the ZIP in Cloud Shell

```bash
cd ~
unzip ModelSpec.zip
cd ModelSpec
ls -la
```

You should see files like:
```
README.md
pyproject.toml
poetry.lock
src/
tests/
rbac/
docs/
```

#### Step 5.2: Install Python Dependencies
```bash
pip install poetry
poetry install
```

#### Step 5.3: Apply RBAC Permissions
RBAC = Role-Based Access Control. This gives PIQC permission to scan the cluster:

```bash
kubectl apply -f rbac/
```

---

### PHASE 6: Test All PIQC Commands and Save Outputs! 🎉

First, create an output folder:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/piqc-test-outputs
cd ~/ModelSpec
```

---

#### Test 1: Connection Test
```bash
poetry run piqc test-connection | tee ~/piqc-test-outputs/01-connection-test.txt
```
✅ **Expected**: Shows "Connection successful" with cluster info

---

#### Test 2: Basic Scan (Table View)
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format table | tee ~/piqc-test-outputs/02-scan-table.txt
```
✅ **Expected**: A nice table showing the vllm-tinyllama deployment

---

#### Test 3: YAML Output
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format yaml > ~/piqc-test-outputs/03-modelspec.yaml
cat ~/piqc-test-outputs/03-modelspec.yaml
```
✅ **Expected**: YAML document with all collected information

---

#### Test 4: JSON Output
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format json > ~/piqc-test-outputs/04-modelspec.json
cat ~/piqc-test-outputs/04-modelspec.json
```
✅ **Expected**: JSON format output

---

#### Test 5: Save to Output Directory
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format yaml -o ~/piqc-test-outputs/05-output-dir
ls -la ~/piqc-test-outputs/05-output-dir/
```
✅ **Expected**: Files created in the output directory

---

#### Test 6: Verbose Mode (More Details)
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format table -v 2>&1 | tee ~/piqc-test-outputs/06-verbose.txt
```
✅ **Expected**: Extra logging information shown

---

#### Test 7: Debug Mode (Maximum Details)
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format table --debug 2>&1 | tee ~/piqc-test-outputs/07-debug.txt
```
✅ **Expected**: Very detailed debug output

---

#### Test 8: GPU Metrics Collection
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format yaml -v 2>&1 | tee ~/piqc-test-outputs/08-gpu-metrics.yaml
```
✅ **Expected**: GPU info with utilization, memory, temperature

---

#### Test 9: Runtime Metrics (vLLM API)
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --collect-runtime --format yaml > ~/piqc-test-outputs/09-runtime-metrics.yaml
cat ~/piqc-test-outputs/09-runtime-metrics.yaml
```
✅ **Expected**: `runtimeState` section with live metrics

---

#### Test 10: PIQC Facts Bundle
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --output-piqc -o ~/piqc-test-outputs/10-piqc-facts
cat ~/piqc-test-outputs/10-piqc-facts/piqc-facts.json
```
✅ **Expected**: JSON file with facts like `runtime.engineType`, `hardware.gpuType`

---

#### Test 11: Scan All Namespaces
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --format table | tee ~/piqc-test-outputs/11-all-namespaces.txt
```
✅ **Expected**: Scans all namespaces, finds the inference workload

---

#### Test 12: Combined Output (Single File)
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --combined --format yaml -o ~/piqc-test-outputs/12-combined
ls -la ~/piqc-test-outputs/12-combined/
```
✅ **Expected**: Single combined YAML file for all deployments

---

#### Test 13: Disable GPU Metrics (Faster Scan)
```bash
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --no-exec --format yaml > ~/piqc-test-outputs/13-no-gpu.yaml
```
✅ **Expected**: Faster scan, but no GPU utilization data

---

#### Test 14: Check Version
```bash
poetry run piqc version | tee ~/piqc-test-outputs/14-version.txt
```
✅ **Expected**: Shows version information

---

### List All Generated Outputs

```bash
echo "=== All Generated Output Files ===" | tee ~/piqc-test-outputs/00-summary.txt
ls -la ~/piqc-test-outputs/ | tee -a ~/piqc-test-outputs/00-summary.txt
echo "" >> ~/piqc-test-outputs/00-summary.txt
echo "=== File Sizes ===" >> ~/piqc-test-outputs/00-summary.txt
du -h ~/piqc-test-outputs/* >> ~/piqc-test-outputs/00-summary.txt
```

---

### Download All Outputs (Cloud Shell)

#### Step 1: Create a ZIP of all outputs

```bash
cd ~
zip -r piqc-test-outputs.zip piqc-test-outputs/
ls -la piqc-test-outputs.zip
```

#### Step 2: Download the ZIP

1. In Cloud Shell, click the **three dots menu (⋮)** in the top-right corner
2. Click **"Download"**
3. Type: `piqc-test-outputs.zip`
4. Click **"Download"**
5. Save to your Windows Desktop

#### Step 3: Extract on Windows

1. Go to your Downloads folder
2. Right-click `piqc-test-outputs.zip`
3. Select **"Extract All..."**
4. Choose where to extract

---

### Output Files Summary

| File | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `01-connection-test.txt` | Cluster connection test results |
| `02-scan-table.txt` | Table format scan output |
| `03-modelspec.yaml` | Full ModelSpec in YAML |
| `04-modelspec.json` | Full ModelSpec in JSON |
| `05-output-dir/` | Directory with YAML files |
| `06-verbose.txt` | Verbose mode output |
| `07-debug.txt` | Debug mode output |
| `08-gpu-metrics.yaml` | Scan with GPU metrics |
| `09-runtime-metrics.yaml` | Runtime metrics from vLLM API |
| `10-piqc-facts/piqc-facts.json` | PIQC facts bundle |
| `11-all-namespaces.txt` | Full cluster scan |
| `12-combined/` | Combined output directory |
| `13-no-gpu.yaml` | Scan without GPU metrics |
| `14-version.txt` | Version information |

