## 📊 Feature Verification Checklist

After testing, verify these features work:

| Feature | Command to Verify | What to Look For |
|---------|-------------------|------------------|
| vLLM Detection | See Test 2 | `engine.name: vllm` |
| Model Name | See Test 3 | `model.name: TinyLlama...` |
| GPU Metrics | See Test 8 | GPU utilization percentage |
| Precision | See Test 3 | `inference.precision: float16` |
| Max Model Len | See Test 3 | `inference.maxModelLen: 2048` |
| Tensor Parallel | See Test 3 | `inference.tensorParallelSize: 1` |
| Runtime Metrics | See Test 9 | `runtimeState.vllm...` |
| PIQC Facts | See Test 10 | Facts bundle JSON |

---

## 🧹 PHASE 7: CLEANUP (VERY IMPORTANT!)

> ⚠️ **DO THIS TO STOP BILLING!** The cluster costs money every minute it runs.

#### Step 7.1: Delete the vLLM Deployment
```bash
kubectl delete -f vllm-tinyllama.yaml
```

#### Step 7.2: Delete the Cluster
```bash
gcloud container clusters delete piqc-test --zone=us-central1-a --quiet
```

> ⏱️ **Wait**: This takes 3-5 minutes

#### Step 7.3: Verify Deletion
```bash
gcloud container clusters list
```
✅ **Expected**: Empty list or "Listed 0 items"

---

## 💻 ALTERNATIVE: Test from Your Windows Laptop (Remote Testing)

Instead of using Cloud Shell, you can run PIQC directly from your Windows laptop. This connects to the GKE cluster remotely.

> ⚠️ **Prerequisite**: The GKE cluster must already be created (Phase 3)

---

### Step R1: Install Required Tools on Windows

#### 1. Install Google Cloud CLI

1. Download the installer from: **https://cloud.google.com/sdk/docs/install**
2. Run the installer (GoogleCloudSDKInstaller.exe)
3. Follow the prompts (accept defaults)
4. **Check "Run gcloud init"** at the end
5. **Restart your terminal** after installation

#### 2. Verify Installation

Open **PowerShell** (search for "PowerShell" in Start menu) and run:

```powershell
gcloud version
```

You should see version information.

#### 3. Install kubectl

In PowerShell:

```powershell
gcloud components install kubectl
```

Type `Y` when prompted.

#### 4. Install Python 3.11+ (if not installed)

1. Download from: **https://www.python.org/downloads/**
2. Run the installer
3. **✅ CHECK "Add Python to PATH"** (very important!)
4. Click "Install Now"

Verify:
```powershell
python --version
```

#### 5. Install Poetry

```powershell
pip install poetry
```

---

### Step R2: Authenticate with GCP

```powershell
# Login to Google Cloud (opens browser)
gcloud auth login
```

A browser window opens - sign in with the account that has GCP access.

```powershell
# Set the project (replace with actual project ID)
gcloud config set project YOUR_PROJECT_ID
```

---

### Step R3: Get Cluster Credentials (kubeconfig)

This downloads the connection info for your cluster:

```powershell
gcloud container clusters get-credentials piqc-test --zone=us-central1-a
```

**Verify connection:**
```powershell
kubectl get nodes
```

✅ **Expected**: Shows your cluster node with T4 GPU

---

### Step R4: Navigate to Your Project

```powershell
# Go to ModelSpec folder
cd C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\ModelSpec

# Install dependencies (first time only)
poetry install
```

---

### Step R5: Apply RBAC (One-time)

```powershell
kubectl apply -f rbac/
```

---

### Step R6: Run PIQC Commands and Save Outputs!

First, create an output folder:

```powershell
mkdir C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs
cd C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\ModelSpec
```

Now run all tests:

```powershell
# Test 1: Connection Test
poetry run piqc test-connection | Tee-Object -FilePath C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\01-connection-test.txt

# Test 2: Basic Scan (Table View)
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format table | Tee-Object -FilePath C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\02-scan-table.txt

# Test 3: YAML Output
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format yaml > C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\03-modelspec.yaml

# Test 4: JSON Output
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format json > C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\04-modelspec.json

# Test 5: Save to Output Directory
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format yaml -o C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\05-output-dir

# Test 6: Verbose Mode
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format table -v 2>&1 | Tee-Object -FilePath C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\06-verbose.txt

# Test 7: Debug Mode
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format table --debug 2>&1 | Tee-Object -FilePath C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\07-debug.txt

# Test 8: GPU Metrics
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --format yaml -v 2>&1 | Tee-Object -FilePath C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\08-gpu-metrics.yaml

# Test 9: Runtime Metrics
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --collect-runtime --format yaml > C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\09-runtime-metrics.yaml

# Test 10: PIQC Facts Bundle
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --output-piqc -o C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\10-piqc-facts

# Test 11: All Namespaces
poetry run piqc scan --format table | Tee-Object -FilePath C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\11-all-namespaces.txt

# Test 12: Combined Output
poetry run piqc scan --combined --format yaml -o C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\12-combined

# Test 13: No GPU Metrics
poetry run piqc scan --namespace inference --no-exec --format yaml > C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\13-no-gpu.yaml

# Test 14: Version
poetry run piqc version | Tee-Object -FilePath C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs\14-version.txt
```

**View all output files:**
```powershell
dir C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\piqc-test-outputs
```

All files are saved on your Desktop in `piqc-test-outputs` folder!

---

### Step R7: Using Custom Kubeconfig (Optional)

If you have the kubeconfig in a custom location:

```powershell
# Specify custom kubeconfig path
poetry run piqc scan --kubeconfig C:\path\to\kubeconfig --format table

# Specify a specific context
poetry run piqc scan --context gke_YOUR_PROJECT_us-central1-a_piqc-test --format table
```

**To see available contexts:**
```powershell
kubectl config get-contexts
```

---

### Windows Remote Testing Checklist

| Step | Command | Expected Result |
|------|---------|-----------------|
| Check gcloud | `gcloud version` | Shows version info |
| Check kubectl | `kubectl version --client` | Shows client version |
| Check Python | `python --version` | Shows Python 3.11+ |
| Check connection | `kubectl get nodes` | Shows cluster nodes |
| Check PIQC | `poetry run piqc version` | Shows PIQC version |
| Test scan | `poetry run piqc scan -n inference` | Shows vLLM deployment |

---

### Troubleshooting Windows Remote Connection

**"gcloud is not recognized"**
- Restart PowerShell after installing gcloud
- Or run the installer again and make sure "Add to PATH" is selected

**"Unable to connect to the server"**
```powershell
# Re-authenticate
gcloud auth login
gcloud container clusters get-credentials piqc-test --zone=us-central1-a
```

**"The connection to the server was refused"**
- Make sure the cluster is still running:
```powershell
gcloud container clusters list
```

**"Error: You must be logged in to the server"**
```powershell
# Get fresh credentials
gcloud auth application-default login
```

**"poetry is not recognized"**
```powershell
# Reinstall poetry
pip install poetry

# Or try with full path
python -m poetry run piqc scan --format table
```

---

